<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>{{ title }}</title>
<style>
  body { font-family: -apple-system, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif; line-height: 1.5;
         max-width: 920px; margin: 2rem auto; padding: 0 1rem; color: #222; }
  h1 { font-size: 1.6rem; }
  h2 { font-size: 1.2rem; margin-top: 2rem; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; padding-bottom: 0.2rem; }
  table { border-collapse: collapse; margin: 0.5rem 0; }
  th, td { border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 0.3rem 0.6rem; text-align: left; }
  th { background: #f4f4f4; }
  .recommendation { background: #f7f9fb; border-left: 4px solid #4477aa; padding: 0.8rem 1rem; }
  .warning { color: #aa5500; }
  img { max-width: 100%; height: auto; }
  .meta { color: #666; font-size: 0.85rem; }
  code { background: #f0f0f0; padding: 0 0.2rem; }
</style>
</head>
<body>
<h1>{{ title }}</h1>
<p class="meta">Generated by beam {{ beam_version }} at {{ created_utc }}.</p>

<div class="recommendation">
<p>{{ recommendation }}</p>
{% if critical_difference and critical_difference.transitivity and not critical_difference.transitivity.is_transitive %}
<p class="warning">Consistency: the pairwise majorities are not transitive
({{ critical_difference.transitivity.n_circular_triads }} circular triad{{ "" if critical_difference.transitivity.n_circular_triads == 1 else "s" }}),
so no single order represents them{% if critical_difference.transitivity.choice %}, though
<code>{{ critical_difference.transitivity.choice }}</code> is the method preferred to every other one
individually{% endif %}. See the critical-difference section below.</p>
{% endif %}
</div>

<h2>Input</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th>layout</th><td>{{ layout }}</td></tr>
  <tr><th>tools</th><td>{{ n_tools }}</td></tr>
  <tr><th>metrics</th><td>{{ metric_ids | join(", ") }}</td></tr>
  {% if dataset_names %}<tr><th>datasets</th><td>{{ dataset_names | length }}: {{ dataset_names | join(", ") }}</td></tr>{% endif %}
  {% if source_path %}<tr><th>source</th><td><code>{{ source_path }}</code></td></tr>{% endif %}
  <tr><th>weighting</th><td>{{ weighting }}</td></tr>
  <tr><th>aggregation</th><td>{{ method }}</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Normalization</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th>metric</th><th>strategy</th></tr>
  {% for row in normalization_rows %}
  <tr><td>{{ row.metric }}</td><td>{{ row.strategy }}</td></tr>
  {% endfor %}
</table>
{% if warnings %}
<ul>
  {% for w in warnings %}<li class="warning">{{ w }}</li>{% endfor %}
</ul>
{% else %}
<p>No normalization guard warnings.</p>
{% endif %}

<h2>Ranking</h2>
<img src="data:image/png;base64,{{ ranking_figure }}" alt="composite score per tool">
<table>
  <tr><th>rank</th><th>tool</th><th>composite</th></tr>
  {% for row in ranking_rows %}
  <tr><td>{{ row.rank }}</td><td>{{ row.tool }}</td><td>{{ row.composite }}</td></tr>
  {% endfor %}
</table>
<img src="data:image/png;base64,{{ heatmap_figure }}" alt="normalized scores heatmap">

{% if funky_figure %}
<h2>Rank robustness</h2>
<p>Circle size is the normalized score per metric and the bar is the composite. The panels
on the right are each tool's rank span across the leave-one-dataset-out runs, its SMAA rank
acceptability, and its rank span across the five aggregations. A narrow span means a stable
position; a wide one means a position dependent on an analyst choice.</p>
<img src="data:image/png;base64,{{ funky_figure }}" alt="funky heatmap with rank-robustness panels">
{% endif %}

<h2>Sensitivity</h2>
<p>The ranking above uses one weighting ({{ weighting }}) and one aggregation ({{ method }}).
The sections below are the same ranking under the alternative choices.</p>
{% if smaa_figure %}
<h3>SMAA weight sampling</h3>
<img src="data:image/png;base64,{{ smaa_figure }}" alt="SMAA confidence per tool">
<table>
  <tr><th>tool</th><th>ranked first (percent of draws)</th></tr>
  {% for row in smaa_rows %}
  <tr><td>{{ row.tool }}</td><td>{{ row.confidence }}</td></tr>
  {% endfor %}
</table>
{% endif %}
{% if loo %}
<h3>Leave one metric out</h3>
<p>The top-ranked tool kept its rank in {{ loo.top_stability_pct }} percent of the
{{ loo.n_metrics }} leave-one-metric-out runs. Removing <code>{{ loo.most_influential_metric }}</code>
caused the largest rank change, a shift of {{ loo.max_rank_shift }}.</p>
{% endif %}
{% if lodo %}
<h3>Leave one dataset out</h3>
<img src="data:image/png;base64,{{ lodo_figure }}" alt="rank stability per tool across dataset omissions">
<p>The top-ranked tool kept its rank in {{ lodo.top_stability_pct }} percent of the
{{ lodo.n_datasets }} leave-one-dataset-out runs. Omitting dataset
<code>{{ lodo.most_influential_dataset }}</code> caused the largest rank change, a shift of
{{ lodo.max_rank_shift }}.</p>
{% endif %}
{% if aggregation_agreement %}
<h3>Aggregation agreement</h3>
<p>Re-ranking under the {{ aggregation_agreement.n_methods }} aggregations gives orderings that
agree at a mean Kendall tau-b of {{ aggregation_agreement.mean_tau }}.
{% if aggregation_agreement.top_is_unanimous %}Every aggregation ranks
<code>{{ aggregation_agreement.top_tool }}</code> first, so the top recommendation does not
depend on the aggregation choice.{% else %}They do not all rank the same tool first, so the top
recommendation depends on which aggregation is used.{% endif %}</p>
{% endif %}
{% if normalization_agreement %}
<h3>Normalization agreement</h3>
<p>Re-ranking under {{ normalization_agreement.n_labels }} normalizations
({{ normalization_agreement.labels }}) gives orderings that agree at a mean Kendall tau-b of
{{ normalization_agreement.mean_tau }}. The <code>recommended</code> column is the per-metric
default declared on each card ({{ normalization_agreement.recommended_detail }}); the others apply one
strategy to every metric.
{% if normalization_agreement.top_is_unanimous %}Every normalization ranks
<code>{{ normalization_agreement.top_tool }}</code> first, so the top recommendation does not
depend on the normalization choice.{% else %}They do not all rank the same tool first, so the top
recommendation depends on which normalization is used.{% endif %}</p>
<img src="data:image/png;base64,{{ normalization_agreement.figure }}" alt="normalization agreement heatmap">
{% endif %}
{% if rank_sensitivity %}
<h3>Rank variance by factor</h3>
<p>Across the {{ rank_sensitivity.n_combinations }} combinations of weighting, aggregation and
dataset, the rank variance splits into the dataset ({{ rank_sensitivity.dataset_pct }} percent),
the weighting choice ({{ rank_sensitivity.weighting_pct }} percent), the aggregation choice
({{ rank_sensitivity.aggregation_pct }} percent), and their interactions
({{ rank_sensitivity.interaction_pct }} percent). The largest is the
{{ rank_sensitivity.most_influential }}.</p>
<p>The top tool overall (<code>{{ rank_sensitivity.headline_tool }}</code>) ranks first in
{{ rank_sensitivity.headline_top_pct }} percent of combinations, with a rank span of
{{ rank_sensitivity.headline_rank_span }}. Its mean rank per dataset:</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>dataset</th><th>mean rank of {{ rank_sensitivity.headline_tool }}</th></tr>
  {% for row in rank_sensitivity.headline_by_dataset %}
  <tr><td>{{ row.dataset }}</td><td>{{ row.rank }}</td></tr>
  {% endfor %}
</table>
{% if rank_sensitivity.spec_curve_figure %}
<h3>Specification curve</h3>
<p>Each of the {{ rank_sensitivity.n_combinations }} combinations of weighting, aggregation and
dataset is one specification. The most frequent top method
(<code>{{ rank_sensitivity.headline_tool }}</code>) ranks first in
{{ rank_sensitivity.top_first_pct }} percent of them. The single most common ordering of all tools
holds in {{ rank_sensitivity.modal_order_pct }} percent of specifications, and
{{ rank_sensitivity.n_distinct_top }} distinct tool(s) rank first in at least one specification.
The curve below plots that method's rank across the specifications, sorted from its best rank to
its worst; the panel beneath marks the choices of each specification.</p>
<img src="data:image/png;base64,{{ rank_sensitivity.spec_curve_figure }}" alt="specification curve"/>
{% endif %}
{% endif %}
{% if perturbation %}
<h3>Smallest weight perturbation</h3>
{% if perturbation.top_rank_is_fragile %}
<p class="warning">The top rank is fragile: a weight change of about {{ perturbation.delta }}
on <code>{{ perturbation.metric }}</code> overturns it.</p>
{% elif perturbation.delta is defined %}
<p>The top rank is stable: the smallest single-metric weight change overturning it is
about {{ perturbation.delta }} on <code>{{ perturbation.metric }}</code>.</p>
{% else %}
<p>No single-metric weight change within the searched range overturns the top rank.</p>
{% endif %}
{% endif %}

{% if reference_levels %}
<h2>Reference levels</h2>
{% if reference_levels.baseline_rows %}
<h3>Chance baseline</h3>
<p>Number of tools above a random method on each metric with a declared chance baseline.</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>metric</th><th>chance score</th><th>tools above chance</th></tr>
  {% for row in reference_levels.baseline_rows %}
  <tr><td><code>{{ row.metric }}</code></td><td>{{ row.baseline }}</td>
  <td>{{ row.n_beating }} of {{ row.n_observed }} ({{ row.pct }} percent)</td></tr>
  {% endfor %}
</table>
{% if reference_levels.tools_never_beating %}
<p class="warning">Tools without a score above chance on any metric with a declared baseline:
{{ reference_levels.tools_never_beating | join(", ") }}. On this evidence these tools are not
distinguishable from a random method.</p>
{% else %}
<p>Every tool scores above chance on at least one metric.</p>
{% endif %}
{% endif %}
{% if reference_levels.top_pair_indistinguishable is defined %}
<h3>Noise floor</h3>
{% if reference_levels.top_pair_indistinguishable %}
<p class="warning">The top two tools ({{ reference_levels.top_pair }}) differ by less than the noise
floor on every metric that declares one, so the order between them is within measurement noise.</p>
{% else %}
<p>The top two tools ({{ reference_levels.top_pair }}) are separated above the noise floor on at
least one metric.</p>
{% endif %}
{% if reference_levels.indistinguishable_pairs %}
<p>Tool pairs within the noise floor on every metric:</p>
<ul>
  {% for pair in reference_levels.indistinguishable_pairs %}<li>{{ pair }}</li>{% endfor %}
</ul>
{% endif %}
{% endif %}
{% endif %}

{% if card_consistency %}
<h2>Card and data consistency</h2>
<p>Agreement of the raw scores with the values declared on each metric card (range, chance
baseline, target, noise floor), checked before normalization.</p>
{% if card_consistency.violations %}
<p class="warning">The scores contradict the cards in {{ card_consistency.violations | length }}
place(s). A normalization or ranking based on these metrics inherits the error.</p>
<ul>
  {% for message in card_consistency.violations %}<li>{{ message }}</li>{% endfor %}
</ul>
{% else %}
<p>The scores stay within every declared card value.</p>
{% endif %}
{% if card_consistency.notes %}
<p>Observations about this score matrix alone:</p>
<ul>
  {% for message in card_consistency.notes %}<li>{{ message }}</li>{% endfor %}
</ul>
{% endif %}
{% endif %}

{% if critical_difference %}
<h2>Critical difference across datasets</h2>
<p>Friedman test p-value {{ critical_difference.friedman_pvalue }} over
{{ critical_difference.n_datasets }} datasets. The Nemenyi critical difference is
{{ critical_difference.critical_difference }} average-rank units; tools closer than this
are not separable at the chosen level.</p>
<img src="data:image/png;base64,{{ critical_difference.figure }}" alt="average ranks with critical difference">
{% if critical_difference.cliques %}
<p>Groups not significantly different:</p>
<ul>
  {% for clique in critical_difference.cliques %}<li>{{ clique | join(", ") }}</li>{% endfor %}
</ul>
{% endif %}
{% if critical_difference.superiority %}
<p>Effect size: the top-ranked method (<code>{{ critical_difference.superiority.top }}</code>) scores
higher than the next (<code>{{ critical_difference.superiority.runner }}</code>) on
{{ critical_difference.superiority.n_outperformed }} of {{ critical_difference.superiority.n_datasets }} datasets
(probability of superiority {{ critical_difference.superiority.pct }} percent).</p>
{% endif %}
{% if critical_difference.transitivity %}
{% set t = critical_difference.transitivity %}
{% if t.is_transitive %}
<p>Consistency: the pairwise majorities are transitive, so one order is consistent with them
{% if t.coefficient %}(coefficient of consistence {{ t.coefficient }}){% endif %}.</p>
{% else %}
<p>Consistency: the pairwise majorities contain {{ t.n_circular_triads }} circular triad{{ "" if t.n_circular_triads == 1 else "s" }}
out of {{ t.n_triads }}, so no single order agrees with all of them. The reported ranking then depends on the aggregation rule
{% if t.choice %}(though <code>{{ t.choice }}</code> is still preferred to every other method individually){% endif %}.</p>
{% endif %}
<img src="data:image/png;base64,{{ t.figure }}" alt="pairwise dominance matrix ordered by methods outperformed">
{% endif %}
{% if critical_difference.bayesian %}
{% set bayes = critical_difference.bayesian %}
<p>Posterior: under the Bayesian sign test (ROPE {{ bayes.rope }}), the probability that
<code>{{ bayes.top }}</code> is practically better than <code>{{ bayes.runner }}</code> is {{ bayes.p_better }},
and the probability that the two are practically equivalent is {{ bayes.p_equivalent }}.</p>
<img src="data:image/png;base64,{{ bayes.figure }}" alt="posterior probability that the row method is practically better than the column method">
{% endif %}
{% endif %}

<h2>Reproducibility</h2>
<p class="meta">Input sha256: <code>{{ input_sha256 }}</code></p>
<table>
  <tr><th>package</th><th>version</th></tr>
  {% for name, version in software.items() %}
  <tr><td>{{ name }}</td><td>{{ version }}</td></tr>
  {% endfor %}
</table>
<p class="meta">The run manifest written next to this report records the metric card versions
and content hashes.</p>

</body>
</html>
